  "worked_solution": "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{\u2032}(x)$ is **inversely proportional** to the **distance from $P$ to the origin**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *distance from $P(x,y)$ to the origin* is given by $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *inversely proportional* to the distance from $P$ to the origin, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=k\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{k}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}.\n$$",
